WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:07.120
The Strand survived through my grandfather
and my dad's very hard work and their passion for this.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:12.460
And then the city comes, just with one vote,
and takes a piece of it.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.560
They just want to put a bureaucratic noose
me.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:17.500
We're just asking to be left alone.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.800
The Strand is New York City's last great book
store.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.449
A four story literary emporium, crammed with
18 miles of merchandise,

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:32.280
stuffed into towering bookcases, arranged
along narrow passageways.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.920
Nancy Bass Wyden, who owns both The Strand
and the building it inhabits

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.140
is fighting to prevent the city from designating
it a historic landmark.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.920
She says that the effort to preserve the building
could destroy her business.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.440
Once the structure is land-marked, the owner
needs to get permission from the city

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.960
before renovating the interior or altering
the facade.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.140
It would be very difficult to be commercially
nimble if we're land-marked.

00:00:55.149 --> 00:00:58.840
We'd have to get approvals through a whole
committee and bureaucracy

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.180
that do not know how to run a book store.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:07.540
And that does not allow me the flexibility
to change with the changing retail book environment

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:09.690
and to serve our customers.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:14.540
The strand started out as just one destination
along the world famous Book Seller's Row.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:18.340
A commercial district comprised of about 40
secondhand dealers.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:23.280
When Wyden's immigrant grandfather opened
his book stall on 8th Street in 1927

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.120
nobody could have predicted that one day it
would be the district's sole survivor.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:32.060
Wyden's sense of outrage that the city wants
to compromise her property rights

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:37.860
derives in part from her father and grandfather's
decades of struggle to keep the business alive.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.960
The Depression hit, so my grandfather just
reverted to living on a cot

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.580
in the basement of the book store.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:52.100
My grandfather and my dad both worked most
of their life 6 days a week, hardly took vacations,

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:57.540
and to his credit my dad saved up all of his
money to buy the building.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:01.700
If it's not land-marked, there is the chance
that you can be like

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:06.260
'Screw it, I'm, you know what I realize that
I wanna go spend the rest of my life on the  beach.

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:10.539
I'm shutting down the business, I'm selling
the property, and it's gonna get renovated

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:11.660
and that's my right.'

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:16.900
I believe it is my right, I mean after three
generations of sweat and toil

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:20.660
I mean we should have our say on what we're
going to do.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.760
So how is it that Wyden's freedom to do what
she wants with her book store

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:31.150
can be curtailed by eleven un-elected individuals?

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:35.989
Signed into law in 1965, the Landmarks Act
was challenged as unconstitutional

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:39.159
13 years later by the owner of Grand Central
Terminal.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:44.280
Which sued the city for preventing it from
building a skyscraper on top of the train station.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:45.720
The Supreme Court upheld the law,

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.140
setting a precedent that in the dissenting
opinion of Justice William Rehnquist

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.860
undermined constitutional protections.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:52.980
As Rehnquist wrote,

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:57.060
the city had in a literal sense taken substantial property rights from the company

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.320
without offering just compensation as required
by the Fifth Amendment.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:05.099
Since that ruling, the number of land mark
properties in New York has more than doubled

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:10.920
to about 36 thousand, encompassing more than
a quarter of all the buildings in Manhattan.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:15.459
When the owners of those properties objected,
their stories rarely garnered attention.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:20.120
One of the differences is that you're The
Strand, and you matter, so people pay attention.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:23.400
Do you feel like this type of story is playing
out on different levels

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:27.560
for property owners or business owners who
might not be as famous as you

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.480
or in places as kind of media centric.

00:03:30.489 --> 00:03:35.959
Yes, I've been able to get more attention
because of that and more public outcry

00:03:35.959 --> 00:03:39.340
and I think that there are other business
owners like me

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:44.480
that end up just kind of getting trapped in
this situation without much of a voice.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.920
In her opposition to land marking The Strand:

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.920
We're just asking to be left alone.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:53.640
Wyden is unknowingly echoing the line of retired
public school librarian Ella Suydam,

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:58.000
owner of a Brooklyn farmhouse that had been
in her family since 1720.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.280
'Who the hell are you to tell me what I can
do with my house?'

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.960
Suydam told the Landmarks Commission in 1980,

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:06.740
Intimidating it's members into backing off.

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:11.520
The Commission waited until Suydam was dead
nine years later, to landmark her property.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:16.660
Most building owners are less successful in
their dealings with the Landmarks Commission.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:19.580
Take this property in Manhattan's meat packing
district,

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:23.680
one of the handful of buildings purchased
in the 1940s by Louis Meilman,

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.680
a Russian born butcher.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:31.820
By 2003, his son and grandsons were planning
to cash in by building a much taller building on the site.

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:33.920
Enter the Landmarks Commission.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:36.440
If the building's become part of a landmark
district

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:39.160
this will essentially eliminate new construction

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:43.500
and eliminate the ability for our family to
utilize the undeveloped air rights.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:46.440
The Meilmans lost their fight later that year.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:51.520
All done with your shopping list, or is this
just for you?

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:52.620
Those are just for me!

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.040
Cool, I love it!

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:57.540
Wyden is committed to preserving her family's
legacy.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:00.000
Happy holidays, thanks for coming here.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.560
She just objects to the loss of control over
her property.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:08.860
You add another component of government, you
add bureaucracy, you add committees,

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:13.740
you add people having opinions about what
we should do inside the store

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:16.220
as well as outside the store.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:20.860
Our family's been a great steward, two years
ago there was a massive sewer fire

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:24.520
and we restored the windows to the prior look

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:29.000
and we restored the granite pillars to the
way it originally had been.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:35.520
I want to continue The Strand forever, that's
my legacy and my goal in life.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:39.100
But can a bricks and mortar book store really
survive forever?

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:42.380
In 1969, at a time when The Strand was thriving,

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:44.200
Wyden's father, Fred Bass,

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:48.200
told a New York Times reporter that obsolescence would come eventually

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:53.160
'Some day' he said 'readers will have a telephone
with a screen and you'll be able to dial a book.'

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:56.160
'Readers will have instant contact with thousands of books.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.840
Then I go into the antiques business.'

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:00.360
You know, he wasn't wrong.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:03.760
Can you imagine a time when The Strand will
come and go,

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:08.040
and you'll be selling antiques or you'll be
selling yoga pads instead.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:15.140
I hope not, I'd like to have a shot at a fourth
generation of my kids running the store and continuing.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:21.030
New York City needs unusual places like The
Strand, with 91 years of history

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:25.320
and for the city to get out of it's way so
it can continue.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:31.600
Do you think you're going to win your fight?

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:36.720
I've been told that nobody wins with Landmarks,

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:42.810
but I want to fight them because it's just
so wrong and so unjust and so unfair

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:46.919
and we can't let them keep running over everybody
in their way.

